fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1077", description: some(value: "A PARAMETER AN OPERATION MERELY ACCEPTS CANNOT BE TOLD FROM ONE IT RECEIVES ON — the residue of WI-1076, and it needs surface syntax.\n\n`spec_carrier_param` (kb/typing.rs, WI-1076) identifies a spec's carrier parameter\nas the first declared type parameter some declared operation TAKES AS A PARAMETER.\n\"Takes\" is WIDER than \"receives on\", which is what should decide. All seven of\nWI-1076's mis-filed stdlib provisions are now closed — six by that predicate and\nthe seventh by repairing a DECLARATION (see below) — so what remains is a shape\nthe corpus does not currently contain but a user can write today:\n\n  (1) A SPEC THAT ACCEPTS ITS OWN ELEMENT.\n      `Set.insert(s: Set, x: T)` and `Map.put(m: Map, key: K, value: V)` receive on\n      the sort and also take the element, so `T` / `K` answers as the carrier\n      parameter. A user writing `sort IntSet provides Set[T = Int64]` gets the\n      provision filed at `Int64` and no inferred default row. No stdlib provision of\n      either exists, so nothing measures it today.\n\nTHE SEVENTH ROW WAS A BUG IN THE DECLARATION, NOT A LIMIT OF THE PREDICATE, and\nthat is the repair to prefer wherever it applies. `LogicalStream.pure` read\n`pure(x: T) -> LogicalStream`, reusing the SORT's element parameter for a value it\nmerely lifts. As `pure[A](x: A) -> LogicalStream[A, {}]` the question stops being\nasked and `Relation provides LogicalStream` reads its provider. The bare return was\nindependently lossy: it expands to `LogicalStream[T = ?, E = ?]` with a FRESH\nvariable, so lifting an `Int64` into a `String`-element stream LOADED CLEAN\n(measured; pinned by `pures_own_type_parameter_threads_its_argument_type`).\n\nIT WAS WRONG FROM THE FIRST DAY, and the vintage is not the excuse it looks like.\n`pure` was written 2026-02-23 (`9e6b81c1`) and the first OPERATION type parameter\nlanded 2026-06-10 (WI-424), but a shared LOGICAL VARIABLE said the same thing and\nneeded nothing new: `pure(x: ?A) -> LogicalStream[?A, {}]` type-checks today\n(measured — same refusal as the bracket form), and the neighbouring `mplus(a:\nLogicalStream{T = ?A}, b: LogicalStream{T = ?A})` was written that way IN THE SAME\nCOMMIT, two lines below. Types are terms and they unify, so operation-level\npolymorphism was always expressible; `pure` reached for the sort's parameter\ninstead. An audit under this ticket should therefore look for the SHAPE, not for\ndeclarations of a certain age.\n\nA NULLARY PRODUCER IS A DIFFERENT CASE AND LOOKS CORRECT AS WRITTEN. `empty()` has\nno argument to relate its result to, so a fresh variable per use IS the polymorphism\nand no parameter is wanted. MEASURED: `List.empty() -> List` (bare, hence\n`List[T = ?]`) loads into `List[T = Int64]` and `List[T = String]` in one program.\n`LogicalStream.empty() -> LogicalStream[?A]` is the same spelling class (§5.6: a\nbare reference, a partial application and an explicit `?` all leave the slot to the\nbody; an OPERATION type parameter is the CALLER's to instantiate and is the loud\n`got unconstrained` when a call pins it from nothing — which a bare `empty[A]()`\nwould be). So `empty` should NOT gain a bracket parameter, and `mplus`'s shared\n`?A` is already the right idiom. The stdlib nevertheless spells `empty` five ways\n— `-> List`, `-> Set`, `-> Map`, `-> LogicalStream[?A]`, `-> C`, `-> SortedSet[T =\nT, O = O]` — and only the last has `pure`'s shape (a member typed at the SORT's own\nparameters); that one is worth checking.\n\nNOT MEASURED, and worth settling here: whether `List[Nothing]` + covariance is the\nintended alternative to fresh-per-use. Both mechanisms exist — `Nothing` is the\ndeclared bottom sort and `fact Covariant(sort: List, param: T)` is asserted in\nreflect/typing.anthill — but `empty` uses unification, not subsumption. In a\nterm-unification setting fresh-per-use needs no variance machinery at all, which is\nan argument for keeping it; the question is whether the two can disagree anywhere.\n\n  (2) CARRIER-PARAMETERIZED SPEC DECLARING ITS ELEMENT FIRST AND ACCEPTING IT.\n      BoxHolder | Holder | Int64          <- explicit `C = Box` DISCARDED\n      `sort Holder { sort Element = ?; sort C = ?; operation peek(c: C); operation\n      has(c: C, e: Element) }` — `has` accepts `Element`, which is declared first, so\n      `Element` answers and the written `C = Box` binding is thrown away. Not a\n      regression (the old positional read answered the same), but it is why WI-1076's\n      \"declaration order retires the positional assumption\" holds only while no\n      operation takes the earlier parameter.\n      Pinned: `an_accepted_element_declared_first_still_wins_over_the_carrier_param`.\n\nWHY THE OBVIOUS FIXES WERE MEASURED AND REJECTED (WI-1076 tried both):\n\n  * `spec_is_self_representing` (WI-614) — \"does any operation take the SORT\n    itself\" — closes (1), and REFUSES A PROGRAM THAT LOADS. A spec may declare BOTH\n    a carrier parameter and a self-receiving operation; gating on\n    self-representation discards its explicit `C = Box` binding and the load fails\n    with a `requires Holder[Element = …]` mismatch. Fixture:\n    `a_spec_with_both_a_carrier_param_and_a_self_receiver_keeps_its_binding`.\n  * \"the op does not RETURN the spec sort\" (producer vs observer) separates `pure`\n    from `peek` on today's corpus but misclassifies a plausible `operation add(c: C,\n    x: Int64) -> Coll` as having no carrier — the UNSAFE direction, since a false\n    no-carrier verdict throws a written binding away while a false carrier verdict\n    merely leaves a provision where it was.\n\nThe sound discriminator already exists and cannot be used here: eval's\n`carrier_param_receiver_for_values` gates on `provision_binds_param_to_carrier` —\n\"does the value's carrier provide the spec with THAT param bound to the carrier\" —\nwhich is provision-relative, so asking it inside `provision_carrier_binding` is\ncircular, and asking it across provisions makes the answer depend on which\nprovisions happen to exist.\n\nWHAT MUST BE DECIDED, and it is a LANGUAGE question, which is why this is a ticket\nand not an inline fix: nothing in the surface says which parameter is a spec's\ncarrier (059's fact-ban section: no `spec` keyword, no `SymbolKind::Spec`; the\nkernel infers spec-hood from shape everywhere). Options, smallest first:\n\n  (a) mark the carrier parameter in the declaration (`sort C = ? carrier`, or a\n      `self` convention) — makes the illegal state unrepresentable and gives\n      `spec_carrier_param` a declaration to read instead of a heuristic;\n  (b) declare spec-hood (`spec Iterable … end`) and derive the carrier from it;\n  (c) leave it inferred and accept the residue, documenting the rule as\n      \"a parameter no operation takes is not a carrier\" — which is what ships today.\n\n(a) and (b) are grammar + loader + spec changes and would let every shape reader in\nkb/typing.rs stop guessing — `spec_is_self_representing`, `spec_carrier_param`,\n`self_receiver_param_index` and `carrier_param_receiver` are four inferences of one\nundeclared fact.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the chosen option is recorded in kernel-language §5.1 (which carries\nthis ticket number at the gap) and in 058 §3.6; both pinned rows above are\nre-measured and updated; `self_provides(Relation, LogicalStream)` holds; the\nmixed-shape and witness controls in `wi1076_self_representing_spec_carrier_test`\nstill pass UNCHANGED — they are what the rejected predicates broke; each test says\nwhich case fails when the change is backed out. cargo-test green via\nrustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1076 (closed six of seven; measured and rejected both alternatives),\nWI-1069 (settled what a provides clause's bindings mean), WI-614\n(`spec_is_self_representing`), WI-424 (`provision_binds_param_to_carrier`, the\nsound but provision-relative gate), WI-860, proposal 058 §3.6, kernel-language §5.1."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-11T23:12:48Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1077", name: "quantifiers")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1077", author: "claude", content: "WHAT THE WI-1079 DEPENDENCY DOES AND DOES NOT GIVE THIS TICKET — correcting a claim made when the edge was added. The dependency was recorded on the reasoning that \"a bounded binder is WI-1077's vehicle\". That was too strong, and the grammar refutes it: `operation_type_param` is `seq(field('name', identifier), optional(seq('=', field('default', _type))))` where the `= default` arm is parsed ONLY to give a good diagnostic (WI-850 refuses it; kernel-language §5.4 says `TypeParam ::= Name`), and `sort_type_param`'s optional list is the HIGHER-KINDED member list `F[T, …]`, not a bound. So NO binder form in the language carries a bound, and WI-1079 does not add one.\n\nBOUNDS EXIST ONLY AT SORT LEVEL and are ALREADY REFLECTED: a sort's `requires Eq[T]` (and the `effects E` desugaring `sort E = ? requires EffectsRuntime[Effects = E]`) is a separate clause emitted as `SortRequiresInfo`. Nothing to add there either.\n\nSO THIS TICKET'S OPTIONS (a) AND (b) STILL REQUIRE NEW SURFACE — `sort C = ? carrier`, or a `spec` keyword. WI-1079 does not deliver the marker; what it delivers is the type-form layer (`PolyType(binders, body)` as a `TypeExtractor` variant, plus `Skolem` / `FlexVar`) that such a marker would be reflected THROUGH. The dependency stands for that weaker reason, and it is a real one: today a declared type PARAMETER reads in reflect as `SortRef(T)`, indistinguishable from a concrete sort — which is precisely the conflation that produced WI-1076's defect (`provision_carrier_binding` filters the binding's base on `SymbolKind::Sort`, and a type parameter PASSES that filter, so `List provides Stream[T]` filed its carrier at `T`). A carrier marker attached to a binder the reflect layer cannot tell from a sort would inherit that same confusion.\n\nALSO: the residue this ticket owns is unchanged in SHAPE but smaller in POPULATION. `LogicalStream.pure` was fixed at the DECLARATION (commit d26bced5) rather than by widening `spec_carrier_param`, which is the repair to prefer wherever a spec reuses its own type parameter by accident. What remains is the INTENDED reuse — `Set.insert(s: Set, x: T)`, `Map.put(m: Map, key: K, value: V)` — plus the element-declared-first face pinned by `an_accepted_element_declared_first_still_wins_over_the_carrier_param`.", at: "2026-08-11T16:50:06Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1077", author: "claude", content: "OPTION (c) IS THE DECISION (user, 2026-08-11): leave the carrier parameter INFERRED and accept the residue, documenting the rule as \"a parameter no operation takes is not a carrier\". No new surface — neither the \"sort C = ? carrier\" marker of (a) nor the \"spec\" keyword of (b).\n\nTHE WI-1079 DEPENDENCY IS THEREFORE REMOVED, and the reasoning is worth keeping because the edge was already on its second justification. It was first recorded as \"a bounded binder is this ticket vehicle\" — refuted by the grammar (no binder form carries a bound; kernel-language 5.4 says TypeParam ::= Name, and WI-850 refuses the parsed \"= default\" arm). It then retreated to \"WI-1079 builds the binder LAYER that such a marker would be reflected THROUGH\". Under (c) there is no marker, so there is nothing to reflect through and the edge has no remaining reason. What is left of this ticket touches no reflect form at all: kernel-language 5.1 and 058 3.6 gain the stated rule, the two pinned rows are re-measured, and WI-1076 controls stay green.\n\nWHAT (c) COMMITS THE LANGUAGE TO, and it should be written down as a RULE rather than as an apology, since a reader hitting the residue needs to know it is intended:\n  * the carrier is the first declared type parameter some declared operation TAKES AS A PARAMETER;\n  * therefore a spec that ACCEPTS its own element (Set.insert(s: Set, x: T), Map.put(m: Map, key: K, value: V)) files its provision at the ELEMENT, not the carrier — \"sort IntSet provides Set[T = Int64]\" is filed at Int64 with no inferred default row;\n  * and a parameter DECLARED FIRST that some operation accepts wins over a later carrier parameter (the shape pinned by an_accepted_element_declared_first_still_wins_over_the_carrier_param).\nThe repair to prefer, wherever it applies, is the one that fixed the seventh row: change the DECLARATION so the spec stops reusing its own type parameter for a value it merely accepts (LogicalStream.pure, commit d26bced5). That is a rule about how to WRITE a spec, and belongs in the same section.\n\nSTILL DOC-ONLY BUT NOT TEST-FREE: the acceptance keeps its measurement half. The two rows above are the only things that pin the residue, and a documented rule with no row driving it is the failure mode this repo names most often.", at: "2026-08-11T21:51:20Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1077", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED as option (c): the carrier parameter stays INFERRED and the rule is written down. No code behaviour changed — (c) adopts what ships — so the deliverable is the RULE stated at every site that answers it, plus the drivers it never had.\n\nTHE FACE NOTHING MEASURED NOW HAS A ROW. wi1077_accepts_vs_receives_test: Bag receives on itself AND takes its own element, so T answers and IntBag provides Bag[T = Int64] files at the ELEMENT; Feeder differs ONLY in returning its element instead of taking one, and files at the PROVIDER. The pair in one program is the rule as a measurement — what moves the carrier is what a parameter list mentions. /code-review ran the back-out both ways and confirmed neither half is a restatement: dropping x: T from Bag.insert flips its row to the provider, adding x: T to Feeder.next flips its row to the element. The other face keeps its wi1076 row, re-worded from \"KNOWN RESIDUE … change this row when WI-1077 lands\" to the shipped rule — what landed KEEPS it.\n\nWHERE THE DECISION IS RECORDED, and the last of these was the review finding worth the pass: kernel-language 5.1 (the TICKET marker is gone), proposal 058 3.6, and — the one I had missed — spec_carrier_param ITSELF, whose doc still read \"that is the shipped approximation rather than the intended rule\" and \"WHAT THIS STILL GETS WRONG, measured and left deliberately — WI-1077\". That is the site all four shape-readers point at, so a reader landing there was told a narrowing fix was owed by a ticket being closed as won't-change. A test cannot catch prose drift; the whole risk of (c) is exactly that drift, so this was not cosmetic.\n\nWHY NARROWING IS NOT AVAILABLE, now stated at the predicate rather than implied: eval's provision_binds_param_to_carrier is provision-relative and circular if asked here; spec_is_self_representing REFUSES A PROGRAM THAT LOADS (a spec may declare both a carrier parameter and a self-receiving operation, and gating on self-representation discards its explicit binding — a_spec_with_both_a_carrier_param_and_a_self_receiver_keeps_its_binding); and a declaration that SAYS which parameter is the carrier is the new surface (c) declines. Where the reuse is ACCIDENTAL the repair is the declaration, as LogicalStream.pure was repaired.\n\nCONTROL, stated at each site: every row here passes with the ticket backed out, because (c) is today's behaviour. What they guard is DRIFT between the documented rule and the implemented one — the only failure mode (c) has. self_provides(Relation, LogicalStream) is asserted against the real stdlib as the case the predicate was fixed FOR, and is flagged at its site as a DELIBERATE re-assertion of a wi1076 row rather than independent coverage.\n\nALSO CORRECTED (review): wi1076's module header still said \"the gap is WI-1077\" and its seventh-row note said \"WI-1077 still owns\" the intended-reuse case; proposal 058's \"Six are closed\" contradicted the line directly beneath it and wi1076's own \"ALL SEVEN are closed\".\n\nRust workspace green (29 binaries).", at: "2026-08-11T23:12:51Z")
